This is Zhi-Wei Sun's Four-Square Conjecture (OEIS A308734), an open research problem for which a $2,500 prize has been offered. It is explicitly marked `@[category research open]` in the problem statement. No proof is currently known to the mathematical community.

The conjecture asserts that every integer $n > 1$ can be written as
$$(2^a \cdot 3^b)^2 + (2^c \cdot 5^d)^2 + x^2 + y^2$$
for nonnegative integers $a, b, c, d, x, y$.

This requires showing that the set of integers representable as a sum of two squares of the special form $(2^a 3^b)^2 + (2^c 5^d)^2$, together with two arbitrary squares, covers all integers greater than 1. The restriction that the first two squares must have bases drawn only from $\{2^a 3^b\}$ and $\{2^c 5^d\}$ respectively is what makes this substantially harder than Lagrange's four-square theorem — the available "first two squares" form a very sparse subset of all squares, and one must prove the residual $n - (2^a 3^b)^2 - (2^c 5^d)^2$ is always a sum of two squares for some choice of $a,b,c,d$.

Despite partial progress (e.g., Banerjee 2024 establishing large-density results and many verified cases), a complete proof for all $n > 1$ is not available in the literature, and I am not able to produce one.

